No, raising `max` won't fix this — it'll just delay the crash.

The log shows `idle=0` and `waiting` climbing steadily for over 2 minutes straight; connections are being checked out and never returned, not just facing heavy legitimate load. That points to `withClient` in db.js:11-16:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  const result = await fn(client);
  client.release();
  return result;
}
```

If `fn(client)` throws, `client.release()` is never called — that client leaks out of the pool permanently. Every failing query eats a connection forever, so the pool drains monotonically until everything's waiting, no matter how big `max` is.

Fix the leak first:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Bump `max` afterward only if `waiting` still climbs under legitimate concurrent load once leaked clients are actually being returned.

